This peer identifies the order on finitary projections with the subsystem order on their fixed neighbourhoods and records the effective part of Scott's exercise.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_le_iff_fixedNbhd_subsystem`).** For two finitary projections on the same neighbourhood system, the first projection lies below the second exactly when the fixed-neighbourhood system of the first is a subsystem of that of the second.

*Proof.* Finitary projections are precisely the fixed points of the operation that extracts the projection generated by fixed neighbourhoods. The order isomorphism between these fixed points and subsystems therefore preserves and reflects the two orders. ∎

**Theorem (`exercise_8_16_assembly`).** The order on finitary projections is characterized by inclusion of their fixed-neighbourhood subsystems, and the fixed-neighbourhood system of every computable approximable map on a computably presented domain is effectively given.

*Proof.* The first assertion is the preceding order characterization; the second is the effective-presentation theorem for fixed neighbourhoods of a computable map. ∎
